



//K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) its breif explanation and examples
Definition
K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) is a supervised machine learning algorithm used for classification and regression tasks.
It predicts the output for a new data point based on the K closest data points (neighbors) in the dataset.
How KNN Works (Step-by-Step)
Choose the number of neighbors K.
Calculate the distance between the new data point and all existing data points.
Select the K nearest neighbors.For:
Classification → Assign the majority class among the K neighbors.
Regression → Take the average value of the K neighbors.
 Distance Formula (Euclidean Distance)
The Euclidean distance between two points is:
d = √((x2 - x1)² + (y2 - y1)²)
This distance helps determine which points are closest to the new data point.
Choosing the Value of K
Small K → More sensitive to noise
Large K → More stable but may reduce accuracy
Common practice: Use odd values of K (for classification) to avoid ties

##Advantages
Simple and easy to understand
No training phase (Lazy Learner)
Works well for small datasets
Can be used for both classification and regression

 ##Disadvantages
Slow for large datasets
Sensitive to irrelevant features
Performance depends heavily on the choice of K
Requires proper feature scaling

## Applications
Medical diagnosis
Recommendation systems
Pattern recognition
Image classification
Fraud detection

## Conclusion

KNN is a simple yet effective algorithm that makes predictions based on the similarity between data points. It is widely used due to its simplicity, but performance depends on selecting an appropriate value of K and proper data preprocessing.
